      },
      "lineNbr": 838,
      "author": {
        "id": 5707
      },
      "writtenOn": "2013-08-27T22:52:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Because I don\u0027t think they are the same.  \u0027is_admin\u0027 gets set to 1 if the admin token is used (really a bootstrapping operation) while role:admin means the admin role has been assigned.  If is_admin is set to 1 then the policy engine is never even called...it is by-passed (this was true before, too) - so there is no point referring to it in policy rules.",
