    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAASn//7cw\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/tests/test_v3_protection.py",
        "patchSetId": 18
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 6486
      },
      "writtenOn": "2013-08-27T21:27:13Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "it looks like someone who shouldn\u0027t know that a user exists or not can tell whether they exist because this returns Forbidden whereas if the user didn\u0027t exist it would return Not Found.\n\nThis should return Not Found so that the server isn\u0027t giving out extra information to an attacker.",
      "revId": "436f08b413cb1df89caeaedfe60b9904ed2fdc37",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "AAAASn//7Dw\u003d",
        "filename": "keystone/tests/test_v3_protection.py",
        "patchSetId": 18
      },
      "lineNbr": 202,
      "author": {
        "id": 5707
      },
      "writtenOn": "2013-08-27T22:52:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So I discussed this with Dolphm and others.  I originally had support for error code obfuscation in this patch, but actually (as you point out) it isn\u0027t that my new code that needs to lie, but rather the standard response from the policy engine (i.e. to pretend it is NotFound).  Not everyone things it is worth doing (UUIDs are hard to guess).  I personally think (and will be proposing a BP for IceHouse) that we ought to have a config switch that \"hardens\" our error codes in this way.  However, I think this is out of scope of the patch here.",
      "parentUuid": "AAAASn//7cw\u003d",
      "revId": "436f08b413cb1df89caeaedfe60b9904ed2fdc37",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    }
  ]
